Monolayer BN - BGW/Epsilon
==========================

In this directory we calculate the RPA dielectric matrix using the
Epsilon code from BerkeleyGW.


Instructions
------------

  1. Look at the WFN file using the utility ``wfn_rho_vxc_info.x`` (you
     will need to ``module load berkeleygw/1.2`` first). What is the
     cell size? What do you think will happen to the number of bands
     needed to converge the calculation if the vacuum is increased or
     decreased?

  2. Take a look at the q-points from ``epsilon.inp``. You should note that
     these q-points match the k-points from the ``WFN`` file, according to
     ``wfn_rho_vxc_info.x``. This is because our ``WFN`` file is Gamma-centered,
     i.e., there is no shift. This way, each q-point, defined as q=k-k', will
     be a k-point in the ``WFN`` file.
  
  3. Why is the first q-point different? Relate the shift of 0.001 to the 
     k-points in the two WFN files. Hint: for q0 points:
     q0 = k_{WFNq} - k_{WFN}

  4. Make sure you understand the flags in ``epsilon.inp``. Since this is a 2D
     system, you should include cell_slab_truncation to use a truncated
     Coulomb interaction to prevent spurious interactions with periodic images.
     Which parameters in ``epsilon.inp`` should be converged? Which convergence
     parameters are coupled?

  5. Run the epsilon calculation using ``./01-run_epsilon.qsub``. This will
     take a few seconds.

  6. Next, let's plot the head of the inverse dielectric matrix. Do this by
     running ``./02-extract_epsinv00.sh``. This script greps for the lines
     where the Head is printed in the output file. Find the block in epsilon.inp
     starting with ' Q0 and |Q0|'. The last column is the norm of |q|, which
     should be the x-axis of your plot.

  7. Look at the file ``head.dat``. The values here are the y-values to use. Make
     a plot. Does the q-grid we are using capture the dip in epsilon inverse?

  8. Your q-grid should be fine enough to capture the variation in
     epsilon^{-1}_00 for small q. What do you think will happen to the dip in
     epsilon^{-1}_00 if the amount of vacuum increases? Will the q-grid need
     to be finer or coarser to capture this behavior?
